WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.675
Rust can enforce memory safety, but it
cannot know whether an email address,

2
00:00:03.755 --> 00:00:07.178
file path, API parameter, tenant ID,
serialized message, environment variable,

3
00:00:07.258 --> 00:00:09.490
or command-line option is valid for the
application.

4
00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.505
Validity is a business and security
decision.

5
00:00:11.585 --> 00:00:14.165
The application has to define it and
enforce it consistently.

6
00:00:14.245 --> 00:00:17.469
External input should be treated as
untrusted until it is parsed, validated,

7
00:00:17.549 --> 00:00:20.178
and converted into a type that represents
the intended domain.

8
00:00:20.258 --> 00:00:22.441
That input may arrive through HTTP
requests, files,

9
00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:25.746
command-line arguments, environment
variables, queues, serialized data,

10
00:00:25.826 --> 00:00:27.116
databases, or other services.

11
00:00:27.196 --> 00:00:30.668
Internal systems can also produce invalid
data when versions drift or assumptions

12
00:00:30.748 --> 00:00:31.095
change.

13
00:00:31.175 --> 00:00:34.152
Parsing should happen at clear boundaries,
close to where data enters.

14
00:00:34.232 --> 00:00:37.704
Defensive code avoids letting raw strings
or unchecked values travel deep into the

15
00:00:37.784 --> 00:00:40.563
application and then become file paths,
authorization subjects,

16
00:00:40.643 --> 00:00:43.917
resource selectors, database query inputs,
downstream request parameters,

17
00:00:43.997 --> 00:00:45.683
or security-sensitive configuration.

18
00:00:45.763 --> 00:00:47.946
The sooner raw input becomes validated
domain data,

19
00:00:48.026 --> 00:00:49.713
the simpler the rest of the code becomes.

20
00:00:49.793 --> 00:00:52.273
Validation should include shape, length,
allowed values,

21
00:00:52.353 --> 00:00:55.231
encoding expectations, resource ownership,
and contextual rules.

22
00:00:55.311 --> 00:00:58.039
A path-like value may need different
checks than a display label.

23
00:00:58.119 --> 00:01:00.798
A tenant ID may need to match the
authenticated subject's scope.

24
00:01:00.878 --> 00:01:04.450
A serialized message may need
version-aware validation before it influences state.

25
00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.018
Clear rejection behavior matters.

26
00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:08.082
Invalid input should be rejected
predictably,

27
00:01:08.162 --> 00:01:11.784
without falling back to risky defaults or
exposing excessive implementation detail.

28
00:01:11.864 --> 00:01:14.096
Defensive Rust code makes trust boundaries
visible,

29
00:01:14.176 --> 00:01:15.763
validates data before sensitive use,

30
00:01:15.843 --> 00:01:18.820
and turns successful validation into
simpler, safer downstream APIs.
